To setup:

1. install RVM.
2. cd to the app's directory, RVM should detect the necessary gemset.
3. `gem install bundler` and then `bundle install`.
4. `bundle exec rackup -E development -o 127.0.0.1 -p 4567`

Open http://127.0.0.1:4567 in your browser. Stop the server with Ctrl+C.

macOS: Ruby installation fails while compiling OpenSSL

If Ruby 3.4.2 fails to compile and make.log mentions OpenSSL 4 headers or
"incomplete definition of type", explicitly select Homebrew's OpenSSL 3:

    brew install openssl@3 libyaml readline zlib
    source "$HOME/.rvm/scripts/rvm"
    rvm install ruby-3.4.2 --with-openssl-dir="$(brew --prefix openssl@3)" --with-opt-dir="$(brew --prefix openssl@3):$(brew --prefix libyaml):$(brew --prefix readline):$(brew --prefix zlib)" --autolibs=read-only
    rvm use ruby-3.4.2@benchmark-facade --create
    gem install bundler -v 4.0.24 --no-document
    bundle install
    bundle exec rackup -E development -o 127.0.0.1 -p 4567

Run these commands from the app's directory. Use openssl@3 explicitly: the
unversioned Homebrew openssl formula may point to OpenSSL 4. The Bundler version
above matches Gemfile.lock.

To run the tests and generate local coverage:

1. Run `bundle exec rake test`.
2. Open `coverage/index.html` in a browser to view the coverage report.

The test task runs Minitest and the JavaScript tests. JavaScript tests use Node.js's
built-in test runner and require no npm packages. Ensure a recent Node.js version
(22 or later) is on PATH inside the test environment. If `node` is unavailable,
only the JavaScript tests are skipped, with a warning. JavaScript test failures
fail the task when Node.js is available.

Run `bundle exec rake test:ruby` or `bundle exec rake test:javascript` to run either
suite separately. The SimpleCov report covers Ruby code only.
